-- title:
--   Γₜ-equivariance of the fine family Theta_f
-- statement:
--   The setting is the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison between a moduli problem of fake elliptic curves with level structure in characteristic-$r$ deformation theory and the Drinfeld formal upper half plane, and the statement asserts one equivariance property of a family of points $\Theta_f$ of the fine moduli scheme.
--
--   **Arithmetic data.** Primes $r$, $\bar r$ with $\bar r \ne r$, and a non-zero natural number $N$ which is squarefree and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$ (`hrr`, `hrN`, `hrbarN`, `hN`).
--
--   **The coefficient rings.** $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), $\pi \in \mathcal O$ is irreducible, $\mathcal O$ is $(\pi)$-adically complete, $\#(\mathcal O/\pi) = r$ and $(r) = (\pi)$ (`hres`, `hunr`); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Next, $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ (written `Onr`) is a characteristic-zero domain over $\mathcal O$ with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $F_r$, such that $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$, that ideal is maximal, every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $F_r(x) \equiv x^r \pmod \pi$ (`hFr`); thus $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ plays the role of the completion of the maximal unramified extension with its Frobenius. Finally $\mathrm{vdet} : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is a homomorphism characterised by $\mathrm{vdet}(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^n$ for some $u \in \mathcal O^\times$ (`hvdet`).
--
--   **The indefinite side.** Rationals $a, b$ with $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ indefinite ($0 < a$ or $0 < b$) and such that, for a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$, the completion $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$ (`hB`); $\Lambda$ is a maximal order in it. A scheme $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with a point assignment `pt` for fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level $N$ over arbitrary commutative rings, satisfying `IsCoarseModuli` (isomorphism invariance, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on algebraically closed fibres, and the universal property). A level $n$ with $3 \le n$, $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N)=1$; a scheme $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with a point assignment `ptF` for pairs (fake elliptic curve, full level-$n$ structure) satisfying `IsFineModuli` (isomorphism invariance, base change, and bijectivity of `ptF` over every base); a group $G$ acting on $M$ by $\rho$ with labels $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ satisfying `IsLevelTwistAction` (the automorphisms $\rho(g)$ lie over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, twisting a full level structure by $\chi(g)$ is implemented by $\rho(g)$ on fine moduli points, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, surjective and injective modulo $n$). A morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ (`hp`), invariant under all $\rho(h)$ (`hρp`), and compatible with the two point assignments (`hp_pt`).
--
--   **Hecke and Atkin–Lehner structure on the indefinite side.** For each prime $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$ a scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with a point assignment `ptT` for curves with an extra $\ell$-level subgroup satisfying `IsCoarseModuliT`, together with two degeneracy morphisms $d_0(\ell), d_1(\ell) : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ computing, on points, the source curve and any curve $\ell$-isogenous to it through the extra level (`hd₀`, `hd₁`); and endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ implementing the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on points (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **The definite side.** Rationals $a_1, b_1$ with $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$ and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \otimes \mathbb Q_v$ a division algebra exactly at the places above $\bar r$ (`hdef`); a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$) with $R_1 \le \Lambda_1$; an adelic unit $n_1$ in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ such that $\mathrm{meetOrder}(R_1,n_1) = R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is Eichler of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`); an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding $\iota_0 : \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$; the place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ above $r$. A subgroup $\Gamma_t \le \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ equal to the group of units integral at every place other than $v$ (`hΓt`, i.e. [`CerednikDrinfeld.CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v`](def/CerednikDrinfeld_CosetGraphAtPrime.html#L37)); families $s_\ell \in \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ and $s^f_\ell$ of adelic units indexed by the primes $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$ with `hs` requiring, for each $\ell$, that $s^f_\ell$ has component $s_\ell$ at each place not above $r$, component $1$ at the places above $r$, that the diagonal image of $\ell$ times $(s^f_\ell)^{-1}$ lies in the level Hecke set of $\Lambda_1$ and $\mathrm{meetOrder}(R_1,n_1)$ at $\ell$ when $\ell \mid N$ and in the prime Hecke set of $\mathrm{meetOrder}(R_1,n_1)$ at $\ell$ otherwise, and that $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$; the subgroups $\Gamma_{t,\ell} = \Gamma_t \cap s_\ell \Gamma_t s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`); and a unit $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma_t$ (`hwbar`).
--
--   **Compatibility of $\Lambda$ with the formal module.** All integers lie in $\Lambda$ (`hΛℤ`), and $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to W(\mathbb F_{r^2})^2$ is an order coordinate system (`hcoord`): additive, sending $1$ to $(1,0)$, multiplicative for the twisted product rule involving $r$ and the Witt Frobenius, injective, with dense image in the $r$-adic sense, and matching reduced traces.
--
--   **The special fibre.** A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of dimension $2$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi$ of height $4$ (`hX₀`), two-variable formal coordinates $\theta_0$ on $A_0$, and `hA₀` stating that $\theta_0$ are formal coordinates for the group law of $A_0$ with formal group $X_0.F$ and that the $\Lambda$-action is computed by $\mathrm{coord}$ through the action and uniformiser series of $X_0$. Families $e, e' : \Gamma_t \to \operatorname{End}(A_0)$ over the base (`he`) with degrees $\deg$, subject to the group `hE1`, `hE1mul`, `hE1sc`, `hE1'`, `hE2`, `hE3`, `hE4` (summarised here): $(e\gamma, e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ preserving the level-$N$ structure; $e$ is multiplicative up to multiplication by powers of $r$; rational scalars $\gamma = c$ give $e\gamma$ equal to multiplication by $c$ up to powers of $r$, and conversely $e\gamma$ a multiplication forces $\gamma$ scalar; for every injective ring map $E_0$ from the centraliser of the action and uniformiser endomorphisms of $X_0$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that each $e\gamma$ is induced on formal coordinates by an element $\varepsilon$ of that centraliser with $E_0(\varepsilon) = r^{k_\gamma} g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$; the action of $e\gamma$ on a given full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ is by translation by labels $\mathrm{lab}(\gamma) \in \Lambda$, multiplicative and matching scalars modulo $n$; and every level-preserving $r$-power isogeny of a fake elliptic curve over an algebraically closed field pulled back from $A_0$ comes from some $e\gamma$ up to powers of $r$.
--
--   **The order $R_2$ and its endomorphisms.** A suborder $R_2 \le R_1$ (`hR₂`, `hR₂o`) such that every element of $R_1$ becomes an element of $R_2$ after multiplication by a power of $r$ (`hR₂r`), with $\hat e : R_2 \to \operatorname{End}(A_0)$ over the base (`hê`), subject to `hE5a`, `hE5b1`, `hE5b2`, `hE5b3`, `hE5c`, `hE5d`, `hE5e` (summarised here): each $\hat e(x)$ is additive on points, commutes with the $\Lambda$-action and preserves the level; $\hat e$ is unital and multiplicative, agrees with the $\Lambda$-action on integers, satisfies $\hat e(\bar x)\hat e(x) = [\mathrm{nrd}\,x]$, is compatible with $e\gamma$ when $x = r^k\gamma$ up to powers of $r$, and admits a single $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ conjugating, through any injective $E_0$, the formal action of both the $e\gamma$ and the $\hat e(x)$ into $\iota_0$ up to powers of $r$.
--
--   **The formal moduli package.** A ring map $\iota : W(\mathbb F_{r^2}) \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$; a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/r$ which is special for the structure map induced by $\iota$ (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`); a moduli package $\mathrm{MD}$ over $(r, O^{\mathrm{nr}})$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`); a rule $\eta$ sending rigidified objects over $r$-nilpotent $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-algebras to points of $\mathrm{MD}$, with `hη` in three clauses: over Noetherian bases $\eta$ identifies admissible rigidified objects exactly up to isomorphism, $\eta$ is natural in the base, and every point of $\mathrm{MD}$ is, on a Zariski cover by localisations, of the form $\eta(t)$ for an admissible $t$. An injective $E_0$ from the centraliser of the action and uniformiser endomorphisms of $\Phi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ whose image is commensurable with $M_2(\mathcal O)$: for some $m$, every $r^m A$ with $A \in M_2(\mathcal O)$ is in the image and $r^m E_0(e) \in M_2(\mathcal O)$ for all $e$ (`hE₀`).
--
--   **The Drinfeld comparison.** A family of maps $eD$ from the points of $\mathrm{MD}$'s associated functor $\mathrm{G}$ over Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebras $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent to $(O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} B) \times \Omega_{K_0,\pi}(B)$, natural in $B$ (`hnatD`), bijective (`hbijD`), with first component the structure map $\psi$ (`hfstD`), transforming the $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$-action on $\mathrm{G}$ into the twisted action `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` on the target (`hGLD`), sending $\pi$-translates to the pair obtained by twisting the first component by $F_r$ and leaving the Deligne datum unchanged (`hPiD`), and $\pi$-translates exist (`hPiexD`).
--
--   **Transport data.** A ring map $\kappa : O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}/r$ compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), a natural number $n_0$ and a series $\beta_0$ which is an isogeny from $\Phi$ to $X_0$ pushed along $\kappa$ of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`); and a full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner at $\bar r$ on the special fibre.** A fake elliptic curve $A_{0w}$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi$ together with $a_w, a_w', b_w, b_w'$ and $k_w$ such that $(a_w,a_w')$ realises the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ from $A_0$ to $A_{0w}$ (`hALw`), $(b_w,b_w')$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_w}$ from $A_{0w}$ to $A_0$ (`hBSw`) preserving the level (`hBSwlev`), and `hJOINTw`, which for every injective $E_0$ as above produces a single $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ conjugating the formal action of every $e\gamma$ into $r^{k_\gamma}g\iota_0(\gamma)g^{-1}$ and the formal action of the composite $a_w$ followed by $b_w$ into $r^{k_w'}g\iota_0(\bar w)g^{-1}$.
--
--   **The map $\Xi$ on rigidified curves.** A rule $\Xi$ assigning to each rigidified fake elliptic curve over a Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, relative to an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$, a point of $\mathrm{GPoint}$, subject to: `hΞleg` (over connected $B$ the $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-leg of $\Xi$ is a Frobenius twist $F_r^k \circ \psi$), `hΞnat` (naturality under pullback of rigidified curves), `hΞiso` (invariance under an isomorphism of curves compatible with the rigidifications up to $r$-powers) and `hΞdef` (over connected $B$ with $r$ nilpotent, and given formal module data for the curve, $\Xi$ is computed by $\eta$ applied to an admissible rigidified object $t$ obtained by rigidity transport with a shift $j \le 1$, the $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-leg being $F_r^{-j}\circ\psi$).
--
--   **The matrix $g_0$ and the dictionary hypotheses.** An element $g_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ together with: `heqΓ`, translation of a rigidification by $e\gamma$ corresponds, through $\Xi$, to the action of $r^c\,(g_0\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g_0^{-1})$ for some $c \in \mathbb Z$; `heqW`, an Atkin–Lehner datum at $\bar r$ on a curve over connected $B$ with $\bar r$ invertible, compatible with the given rigidifications via $a_w$ followed by $b_w$, corresponds to the action of $r^c(g_0\,\iota_0(\bar w)\,g_0^{-1})$; `hK1`, over connected $B$ every point of $\Omega_{K_0,\pi}(B)$ is realised by a rigidified curve whose $\Xi$-leg is the prescribed Frobenius twist of $\psi$ and whose image is carried to $(\psi,P)$ by the twisted action of $g_0^{-1}$; `hK2`, two such realisations are isomorphic with matching rigidification data; `hK3`, realisations pull back along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps; `hK4`, realisations lift along a surjection whose kernel has square zero in the stated sense, compatibly with $\Xi$; `hC0e`, existence of a normalised level transport of $P_0$ to a full level-$n$ structure on a realised curve over connected $B$ carrying formal module data; and `hC0u`, its uniqueness on underlying points.
--
--   **The fine family $\Theta_f$ and the level homomorphism.** A family of maps $\Theta_f$ assigning, for every Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, to each triple consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$, a Deligne datum over $B$ and an element of $G$, a $B$-point of $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$; natural in $B$ (`hnat`); with `hG` stating that the morphism induced by $\rho(h)$ sends $\Theta_f(x, gh)$ to $\Theta_f(x,g)$; with `hC2` stating that for realised data $(E,\varrho)$ over $(\psi,P)$ and a normalised level transport $P_n$, the point $\Theta_f((\psi,P),1)$ is the fine moduli point of $(E,P_n)$; and `hC2c` stating that its composite with $p$ is the coarse moduli point of $E$. Finally a group homomorphism $\theta_t : \Gamma_t \to G$ (no further compatibility is imposed on $\theta_t$ itself), and the translation hypothesis `hC3`: for connected Noetherian $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, realised data $(E,\varrho)$ over $(\psi,P)$, $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ and $(\psi',P')$ obtained from $(\psi,P)$ by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$, there is a rigidification $\varrho'$ of the *same* curve $E$ whose $\Xi$-leg is the prescribed twist and which realises $(\psi',P')$ under the twisted action of $g_0^{-1}$, such that (i) any normalised level transports $P_n$ for $\varrho$ and $P_n'$ for $\varrho'$ satisfy $P_n' = \chi(\theta_t\gamma) \cdot P_n$ on underlying points, and (ii) for every prime $\ell \ne r$ invertible in $B$, every extra $\ell$-level structure on $A_0$ stable under $e\gamma$, and every pair of extra $\ell$-level structures $C, C'$ on $E$ compatible through $\varrho$ respectively $\varrho'$ with it, a point of $E$ factors through $C$ if and only if it factors through $C'$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every type $B$ carrying a commutative Noetherian ring structure and an $\mathcal O$-algebra structure, every witness $hB$ that $\pi$ is nilpotent in $B$, every $\gamma \in \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$, every pair $x, x'$ of elements of $(O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} B) \times \Omega_{K_0,\pi}(B)$ and every $g \in G$: if $x'$ is obtained from $x$ by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$, that is if the $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-leg of $x'$ equals the Frobenius twist $F_r^{-\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0(\gamma))}$ of the leg of $x$ and the Deligne datum of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$, then
--   $$\Theta_f\bigl(x',\ \theta_t(\gamma)\,g\bigr) = \Theta_f\bigl(x,\ g\bigr).$$
--
--   This is the equivariance of the fine family of $M$-points constructed from the Drinfeld formal upper half plane under the twisted action of the away-from-$v$ unit group $\Gamma_t$ of the definite quaternion algebra, the action on the $G$-coordinate being by the level homomorphism $\theta_t$; it is the step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation at which the $p$-adic uniformising group is made to act compatibly on both sides. It is used in the identification of the fibres of the fine family, in comparing its values on pullbacks and Frobenius twists, and in the description of equality of its values over algebraically closed fields.
